@prefix : <#> .
@prefix schema: <http://schema.org/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix onto: <../ontology.ttl#> .

<> a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:name "howto/keychain-identity-vs-p12-file-curl.ttl"@en ;
    schema:description "This machine's curl cannot consume a macOS Keychain identity (by name or SHA-1) for --cert — it must be a PEM/P12 FILE PATH — despite curl -V printing (SecureTransport). Do not infer file-less mTLS is available from that banner alone; test it."@en ;
    schema:dateCreated "2026-08-18T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:author <https://linkedin.com/in/kidehen#this> ;
    schema:about :step-bannerMisleading, :step-testResult, :step-whatKeychainActuallyGives,
        :step-privateKeyDoesExistCliUnderreports, :step-securityFrameworkAttempt .

:step-bannerMisleading a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "curl -V printing (SecureTransport) is NOT sufficient evidence that --cert accepts a Keychain identity name/hash directly — the build's actual TLS backend for cert loading can still be LibreSSL/OpenSSL"@en ;
    schema:text """Agent error, 2026-08-18: told the user 'your curl is built against SecureTransport, so --cert accepts a keychain identity name or SHA-1 hash directly — no p12 file and no passphrase at all' based solely on the version banner:
  curl 8.7.1 (x86_64-apple-darwin25.0) libcurl/8.7.1 (SecureTransport) LibreSSL/3.3.6 zlib/1.2.12 nghttp2/1.68.1
This was stated as fact without testing it, and it is WRONG for this specific build: --cert loading goes through LibreSSL, which treats the argument as a file path only. The user correctly pushed back ('You are presenting .p12 credentials whereas keychain access offers an alternative route than .p12 files') before this was tested, which is what surfaced the error.

The (SecureTransport) tag in the banner describes ONE compiled TLS backend option; a libcurl build can link multiple backends or use SecureTransport only for some operations while cert/key loading still routes through the bundled LibreSSL/OpenSSL. Never infer capability from the banner — attempt the operation and read the actual error."""@en .

:step-testResult a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "Empirical test, this machine: curl --cert '<Keychain identity name>' and --cert '<SHA-1 thumbprint>' both fail identically with error 58"@en ;
    schema:text """curl -k --cert "Kingsley Uyi Idehen (Link In Bio 4)" -o /dev/null "https://linkeddata.uriburner.com:5443/DAV/demos/daas/"
  -> curl: (58) could not load PEM client certificate from Kingsley Uyi Idehen (Link In Bio 4), LibreSSL error error:02FFF002:system library:func(4095):No such file or directory, (no key found, wrong pass phrase, or wrong file format?)

curl -k --cert "F4F190BBF8B0F0EED4D38A0C9013E4D57668726E" -o /dev/null "https://linkeddata.uriburner.com:5443/DAV/demos/daas/"
  -> identical error 58

Both the human-readable identity name and the raw SHA-1 thumbprint (the two forms SecureTransport-native curl accepts for keychain-backed --cert) were rejected the same way a nonexistent file path would be. This is conclusive: this curl treats --cert as a filesystem path, full stop, regardless of what looks addressable via `security find-identity -v`.

NOTE for a future re-test: if curl is ever reinstalled/upgraded (e.g. via Homebrew against a build that links Secure Transport for cert loading, not just as a listed backend), this could change. Re-run the exact two commands above before assuming either the old or new behavior."""@en .

:step-whatKeychainActuallyGives a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "What Keychain DOES provide today: the PKCS#12 passphrase, not the certificate/identity — see p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl for the working chain"@en ;
    schema:text "Given the error-58 result, the migration implemented 2026-08-18 uses Keychain for what it can reliably do here: hold the passphrase that unlocks the standing cert.p12 FILE (item uriburner-p12, read via security find-generic-password -s uriburner-p12 -a \"$USER\" -w). The .p12 file itself remains the --cert argument to curl. This was verified end-to-end: openssl unlock succeeded and an authenticated HTTP 200 was obtained against linkeddata.uriburner.com. A genuinely file-less route (Keychain-native identity for TLS) would require a client built against a TLS backend that actually honors it for certificate loading — not this curl — or a small Keychain-native helper (e.g. Security.framework via Swift/PyObjC) replacing curl for these calls. Not attempted; flagged as a possible future improvement only if the user asks for it." ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerBehaviorGapIdentified ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl> .

:step-privateKeyDoesExistCliUnderreports a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "4"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "CORRECTION 2026-09-13: the private key genuinely IS in Keychain — 'security find-key'/'find-identity' just don't surface it; the file-path-only curl conclusion still stands, for a different reason"@en ;
    schema:text """Agent error, 2026-09-13: told the user 'no private key exists anywhere in Keychain' for the agent identity ('Kingsley Idehen's Agent', SHA-1 B95840EF1745B75EFEB91F8BCD1147DE986A19CD — confirmed a match to the known-good agent fingerprint), based on `security find-key -a -t` returning 'SecItemCopyMatching: The specified item could not be found' and `security find-identity -v` (even with `-p ssl-client`) reporting '0 valid identities found'. The user immediately corrected this with a Keychain Access GUI screenshot showing an 'Imported Private Key' (RSA 2048-bit) nested directly under that exact certificate, in the login keychain.

ROOT CAUSE OF THE FALSE NEGATIVE: not established with certainty, but `security find-key`/`find-identity` evidently under-report what the GUI shows — plausibly a per-item ACL on the imported key (set at import time, e.g. via Keychain Access itself) that restricts which callers can even enumerate it, distinct from using it. Do not conclude a private key is absent from CLI enumeration alone when a screenshot or other GUI evidence says otherwise — treat a GUI-provided screenshot as higher-confidence ground truth than `security find-key`/`find-identity` output for existence questions, though NOT for usability (see below).

WHAT STILL HOLDS, RE-VERIFIED FRESH: even with the key confirmed present, `curl --cert "Kingsley Idehen's Agent"` (by label) and `curl --cert "B95840EF1745B75EFEB91F8BCD1147DE986A19CD"` (by SHA-1) both fail identically:
  curl: (58) could not load PEM client certificate from Kingsley Idehen's Agent, LibreSSL error error:02FFF002:system library:func(4095):No such file or directory, (no key found, wrong pass phrase, or wrong file format?)
This is the exact error signature from :step-testResult, now reproduced against a SECOND identity (the agent's, not just the principal's) — confirming the file-path-only conclusion generalizes across identities, not just the one originally tested. The key's presence in Keychain doesn't matter to this curl build: LibreSSL-backed --cert loading never queries Keychain at all, by label, hash, or otherwise.

PRACTICAL UPSHOT, UNCHANGED: the .p12 FILE remains the only thing this curl can load for either identity. A Keychain-native route (Security.framework/NSURLSession via PyObjC or Swift, bypassing curl's TLS stack entirely) is the only way to actually exercise a Keychain-resident identity for a TLS handshake here — see :step-securityFrameworkAttempt for the first real attempt at that route."""@en ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerUserCorrection ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <verified-identity.ttl#workExample-agentKnownValues> .

:step-securityFrameworkAttempt a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "5"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "PyObjC/Security.framework CAN locate and supply the Keychain identity for a TLS handshake — but the connection to linkeddata.uriburner.com:5443 still fails, isolated specifically to the client-cert path"@en ;
    schema:text """Attempted 2026-09-13, a genuinely file-less route bypassing curl entirely: PyObjC (pyobjc-framework-Security + pyobjc-framework-Cocoa, installed via Homebrew's python3.14 in a venv — the system /usr/bin/python3's pip failed building pyobjc-core from source, a clang -Werror issue unrelated to this task) driving `Security.SecItemCopyMatching(kSecClassIdentity, kSecAttrLabel="Kingsley Idehen's Agent", kSecReturnRef=True)` to fetch a SecIdentityRef, then `Foundation.NSURLSession` with a delegate answering the `NSURLAuthenticationMethodClientCertificate` challenge via `NSURLCredential.credentialWithIdentity_certificates_persistence_(identity_ref, None, NSURLCredentialPersistenceForSession)`.

RESULT — PARTIAL SUCCESS, THEN A DIFFERENT FAILURE: `SecItemCopyMatching` succeeded (status=0, real SecIdentity ref returned) — this independently PROVES the private key is genuinely present and Keychain-API-usable, resolving the false-negative from :step-privateKeyDoesExistCliUnderreports. The client-certificate challenge fired and was answered with the identity (`auth_used=True`). But the connection then failed 3 times in a row (URLSession's own internal retry) with identical `NSURLErrorDomain Code=-1005 "The network connection was lost."`, across FOUR variants tried: (a) ephemeral session config + default server-trust handling, (b) ephemeral config + explicit `NSURLCredential.credentialForTrust_()` override (ruling out server-cert-chain rejection as the cause), (c) attempted `HTTP2Enabled=False` via KVC (raised `NSUnknownKeyException` — not a settable key on this OS, attempt inconclusive), (d) `defaultSessionConfiguration()` instead of ephemeral. All four: identical -1005, after the identity was already supplied.

ISOLATION: a plain NSURLSession GET to the SAME host:port (`https://linkeddata.uriburner.com:5443/`) with NO client certificate at all succeeded immediately (`200 OK`, `NSURLSession.sharedSession()`, no delegate needed) — so the failure is specific to the client-certificate TLS path on this server from this Network.framework stack, not a general connectivity/proxy/DNS issue. (Also ruled out: this environment's sandboxed-Bash CONNECT-proxy tunnel — `scutil --proxy` shows no system HTTPS proxy configured, and this test ran fully unsandboxed with direct network access; the CONNECT-tunnel behavior seen in earlier curl traces is specific to sandboxed Bash invocations of curl, not a host-wide requirement.)

WORKING HYPOTHESIS, NOT CONFIRMED: likely the same class of issue as the already-documented 'TLS session reuse trap' in acp-client/SKILL.md (Node's default https agent losing the WebID-TLS session on a follow-up request) — Apple's Network.framework/HTTP-stack may not cleanly support this server's apparent expectation of a post-ALPN or mid-connection client-certificate exchange under whatever protocol version/features it negotiates, where curl+LibreSSL's older/simpler TLS stack does. Not root-caused further given effort already spent; flagged for whoever next has appetite to dig into packet-level TLS tracing (e.g. `SSLKEYLOGFILE` + Wireshark, or `nscurl --ats-diagnostics` against the host) rather than continuing to vary NSURLSessionConfiguration options blind.

PRACTICAL UPSHOT: the Keychain-native route is CLOSER to working than curl's flat file-path-only limitation (it gets past identity lookup and credential supply, which curl never does at all) but is not yet a working replacement for the `.p12` file for actually completing a request against this specific server. The `.p12` file via curl remains the only currently-working path for this campaign's live testing."""@en ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerBehaviorGapIdentified .
